window.TUNMO_SEARCH = [{"title":"Welcome to Tunmo Agent","text":"Connect models, tools, and geospatial workflows. Explore what Tunmo Agent can do and start your first session.","url":"index.html"},{"title":"Welcome to Tunmo Agent › Meet Tunmo Agent","text":" Tunmo Agent is a Node.js / TypeScript Agent process built on Pi. It provides native JSONL RPC, tool extensions, and managed runtimes for clients. The client handles interaction and task scheduling; the Agent handles sessions, tool execution, and resource lifecycles. You can ask the Agent to research information, read web pages, and keep working toward a goal. You can also install GIS modules as needed to process geospatial data in your project. ","url":"index.html#section-1"},{"title":"Welcome to Tunmo Agent › Start here","text":" 01 / GET STARTED Start your first session ↗ Set up your environment, start the process, and send your first message. 02 / CAPABILITIES Explore built-in capabilities ↗ Web search, Goal mode, and reusable Skills. 03 / GEOSPATIAL Enable GIS modules ↗ Learn about installation confirmation, available tools, and data output. 04 / DEVELOPERS Connect your client ↗ Use native RPC and the separate management interface. ","url":"index.html#section-2"},{"title":"Welcome to Tunmo Agent › Capabilities and availability","text":" Core sessions: Each task has its own Agent process, using native Pi messages and tool events. Browser interaction: Open pages, inspect snapshots, click, fill forms, and capture screenshots; Chrome can be prepared automatically on first use. Built-in capabilities: Web Search, Web Read, Goal mode, and the tunmo-agent / skill-creator Skills. Optional capabilities: QGIS and GeoPandas tools have separate source code and runtimes. Whether they can be installed also depends on the current release configuration and platform. Extension development: Manifest-driven registration, shared runtimes, artifact storage, and execution control. Usage note The default module catalog includes QGIS and GeoPandas. Both are disabled by default; installation must be confirmed and the module enabled separately for each one. See “GeoPandas” and “Version and scope” for the detailed boundaries. Cloud execution currently has only a local simulation backend that must be explicitly enabled. Using optional modules from source requires read access to the private tunmo-plugins repository, or a complete release package from the maintainers that matches your Agent version. Public documentation and the main repository's source code do not mean that all optional resources are public. See Quickstart for setup instructions. ","url":"index.html#section-3"},{"title":"Welcome to Tunmo Agent › Choose your reading path","text":" Users: Start with Quickstart, then learn about the inputs and outputs of web access, Goal mode, and GIS. This documentation describes Agent capabilities; it does not assume that your client already has buttons for them. Client developers: Focus on sessions and RPC, configuration, module management, and troubleshooting. The client is responsible for confirming module installations and creating new sessions. Extension developers: Start with extensions and the SDK, then shared runtimes. Use the complete examples in the repository. ","url":"index.html#section-4"},{"title":"Installation and quickstart","text":"Run the Agent from source and establish a session through standard input and output.","url":"quickstart.html"},{"title":"Installation and quickstart › Set up your environment","text":" Node.js ≥ 24.0.0 and pnpm ≥ 12.0.0 are required. The full offline suite has verified Node 24.0.0, 24.21.0, 25.9.0, and 26.10.0; future untested versions need separate validation. Run the following commands from the root of your local tunmo-agent repository. optional-extensions/ is pinned to a specific commit in the private dezhongtibet/tunmo-plugins repository. Source installation, the optional-module CLI, and a complete build require this content. Obtain read access to the plugin repository and configure your own Git authentication first, then run: git submodule update --init --recursive pnpm install --frozen-lockfile pnpm check:deps Check out the commit pinned by the main repository; do not use --remote to unintentionally upgrade the plugins. GitHub source ZIP downloads do not include submodule files. If you do not have access, ask the maintainers for complete release resources for the matching version. The public main repository alone is not a complete, ready-to-use installation package. Project CI uses a separate read-only TUNMO_PLUGINS_READ_TOKEN ; the default token in a regular fork cannot read this private repository. For a Windows source installation, also run node scripts/build-windows-helper.mjs to generate the process supervisor. Prebuilt releases should include the supervisor for the corresponding platform. Do not mix Agent Node and runtime resources built for different architectures. ","url":"quickstart.html#section-1"},{"title":"Installation and quickstart › Start the process","text":" pnpm start -- --session-id docs-demo-001 --cwd /absolute/path/to/project Replace the example path with your actual project directory. A session ID may contain only letters, digits, periods, underscores, and hyphens, and must start and end with a letter or digit. Assign a unique ID to each client task. Usage note This entry point runs an RPC process, not a terminal chat interface. The launcher trusts the project directory you supply. Project extensions run with the current user's permissions, so use a trusted workspace. ","url":"quickstart.html#section-2"},{"title":"Installation and quickstart › Wait for readiness and send a message","text":" stdout contains one JSON object per line; diagnostics go to stderr. After receiving an extension_ui_request with method: notify and a message containing tunmo-agent ready , write the following lines to stdin: {\"id\":\"state-1\",\"type\":\"get_state\"} {\"id\":\"commands-1\",\"type\":\"get_commands\"} {\"id\":\"prompt-1\",\"type\":\"prompt\",\"message\":\"Introduce the current project and describe the capabilities available to you.\"} Model requests require valid model and credential configuration. Process readiness means initialization is complete; it does not mean provider credentials are available. The client should process responses and streaming events line by line, rather than treating each received data chunk as a complete JSON object. ","url":"quickstart.html#section-3"},{"title":"Installation and quickstart › Next steps","text":" Use get_commands to check for /goal , /tunmo-web-tools , and skill:* commands. Read the browser interaction guide for browser tools and CDP attachment, then the configuration guide to understand data directories, the RPC guide to handle messages and cancellation, and module management to enable GIS. Closing stdin lets Pi exit normally. --offline enables PI_OFFLINE=1 , preventing missing npm/git resources from being fetched at startup. Built-in Web Access also rejects network requests. This is not an operating-system network sandbox, and it does not turn a cloud model into a local model. Offline work still requires resources to be installed and a suitable model to be available. ","url":"quickstart.html#section-4"},{"title":"Configuration and data directories","text":"Understand where model configuration, user resources, and runtime data are stored.","url":"configuration.html"},{"title":"Configuration and data directories › Configuration files","text":" The Agent always uses ~/.tunmo/agent under the current user's home directory. On Windows, it uses the corresponding user home directory. settings.json Pi settings, resource discovery configuration, and tunmo.webAccess search configuration. auth.json Model provider credentials, using the structure supported by the current Pi version. models.json Custom model definitions, using the structure supported by the current Pi version. The first startup creates minimal valid configuration files. Configuration initialization does not supply model credentials. Configure a model through your client's model setup flow or the configuration mechanism for the matching Pi version before sending requests. ","url":"configuration.html#section-1"},{"title":"Configuration and data directories › How damaged configuration is handled","text":" Before startup, the Agent acquires a lock and validates JSON and schemas. A damaged file is renamed to <name>.bak.<timestamp>.<pid> , then replaced with minimal valid configuration. The original contents are preserved so that you can inspect them and recover valid fields manually. Configuration preflight sets directory permissions to 0700 and file permissions to 0600. Actual access control on Windows also depends on filesystem ACLs. After Pi is ready, the client receives configuration repair notices through native notifications. ","url":"configuration.html#section-2"},{"title":"Configuration and data directories › User resources and runtime data","text":" ~/.tunmo/agent/ ├── settings.json / auth.json / models.json ├── sessions/ # Sessions ├── skills/ # User Skills ├── packages/extensions/ # Installed extensions ├── runtime-store/ # Shared runtimes ├── runtime-downloads/ # Download and resume cache ├── optional-modules/ # Installation plans, operations, and health status ├── execution-policy.json # Optional execution policy ├── verification-cache/ # Content verification cache ├── extension-state/ # Indexes, transactions, and leases └── execution-artifacts/ # Persistent artifacts Project workspaces, read-only installation directories, and user data are managed separately. Do not edit shared indexes or delete leases directly. Use the management CLI to enable, disable, uninstall, and reclaim resources. ","url":"configuration.html#section-3"},{"title":"Configuration and data directories › Client integration requirements","text":" Usage note The management CLI supports --agent-dir , but the current Agent session launcher has no corresponding configurable directory argument. Pointing only the management CLI at Electron userData does not change the Agent's data location. The management CLI and the Agent must use the same user's data directory, matching release resources, and the same installation state. Valid extensions, skills, prompts, themes, and packages configuration continues to be handled by Pi's automatic discovery. Model switching retains Pi's persistence semantics: if multiple processes change the default model concurrently, the last write wins. ","url":"configuration.html#section-4"},{"title":"Web search and page reading","text":"Discover sources, then read their contents. Search summaries and page text are provided by separate tools.","url":"web-access.html"},{"title":"Web search and page reading › Search the web","text":" web_search accepts a required query and optional maxResults (1–20), and returns titles, URLs, and summaries. The default is 5 results. {\"query\":\"QGIS coordinate reference system documentation\",\"maxResults\":5} This is a tool-argument example, not a command to write directly to RPC. Users describe what they need in a normal prompt; the model chooses and calls the tool. ","url":"web-access.html#section-1"},{"title":"Web search and page reading › Read a web page","text":" web_read accepts a url and optional maxChars (minimum 200). It supports HTTP/HTTPS text, HTML, JSON, XML, and similar content. HTML is extracted as readable text; the tool does not execute page JavaScript or provide browser interaction. For JavaScript-driven pages, clicks, and form entry, see browser interaction . {\"url\":\"https://example.com/\",\"maxChars\":12000} The default text limit is 40,000 characters, and the default response limit is 5 MiB. Results include the final URL, redirects, status code, byte count, and truncation indicators. Do not treat a truncated result as the full page. Binary formats such as PDFs and images are outside the scope of this reader. For JavaScript-driven pages, clicks, and form entry, see browser interaction . ","url":"web-access.html#section-2"},{"title":"Web search and page reading › Configure a search service","text":" SearXNG, Brave, and Tavily are supported. By default, the tool uses the Tunmo SearXNG address configured in the code, with no search API key required. Actual availability still depends on network connectivity and service status. { \"tunmo\": { \"webAccess\": { \"provider\": \"searxng\", \"searxngUrl\": \"https://search.example.com/\", \"timeoutMs\": 20000, \"maxBytes\": 5242880, \"maxResults\": 5 } } } Replace the address with a real SearXNG instance and merge this into your existing settings without overwriting other configuration. Environment variables take precedence over the configuration file. Create a new session after making changes. TUNMO_WEB_SEARCH_PROVIDER : The provider. TUNMO_WEB_SEARCH_API_KEY : A shared credential setting that takes precedence over provider-specific variables. BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY / TAVILY_API_KEY : Provider credentials. TUNMO_WEB_SEARXNG_URL : The instance's base URL, also used for the configured fallback. TUNMO_WEB_TIMEOUT_MS : 1,000–120,000 milliseconds. TUNMO_WEB_MAX_BYTES : The web_read response limit (1 KiB–50 MiB); search requests still use the default 5 MiB limit. TUNMO_WEB_MAX_RESULTS : The number of search results (1–20). The default SearXNG address in the current code uses HTTP. Deployments that require transport security should configure an accessible HTTPS search endpoint. If Brave / Tavily fails, the tool may fall back to the configured SearXNG instance. Before use, confirm that search terms may be sent to that service. This page documents the configuration contract; it does not claim that any hosted service has been verified as online. timeoutMs is the timeout for an individual fetch request. Provider fallback can add to the elapsed time, so it is not an overall deadline for the entire search. ","url":"web-access.html#section-3"},{"title":"Web search and page reading › Access boundaries and troubleshooting","text":" Loopback, private-network, and cloud metadata addresses are rejected by default. TUNMO_WEB_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORK=1 is for testing only, not regular configuration. Use /tunmo-web-tools to inspect tool and configuration status. On failure, check the structured details and error type. Example prompt: Find the official documentation for projected coordinate systems, read the relevant pages, and include source links. With PI_OFFLINE=1 (or the --offline startup option), Web Access returns Offline . URL, redirect, and DNS address checks define the boundaries of these web tools; they do not provide system-wide network isolation for all extensions and Python scripts. The current implementation checks DNS results before fetch establishes a connection. The two operations are not bound to the same IP address, so this must not be described as complete protection against DNS rebinding. DNS lookup is not connected to the cancellation signal, and the tools do not enforce a request rate limit. Timeouts, response size, and result count are separate limits. ","url":"web-access.html#section-4"},{"title":"Browser interaction","text":"Open pages, inspect interactive snapshots, click, fill forms, and capture screenshots with local or attached browsers.","url":"browser.html"},{"title":"Browser interaction › Browser tools and page reading","text":" The browser tools come from project-local pi-agent-browser-native and agent-browser dependencies. At startup, the Agent loads the native Pi extension and command from the current installation; no global installation is needed. agent_browser opens pages, captures interactive snapshots, clicks, fills forms, and takes screenshots. agent_browser_code handles loops and branches, while agent_browser_tools lists and enables specialized tools. {\"args\":[\"open\",\"https://example.com\"]} {\"args\":[\"snapshot\",\"-i\"]} These are tool argument examples for model-driven prompts, not RPC commands to write directly to stdin. web_read extracts page text without running JavaScript or interacting with the page. ","url":"browser.html#section-1"},{"title":"Browser interaction › First local browser use","text":" When a tool first needs a local browser, the Agent checks for Chrome. If Chrome is absent and the session allows network access, it downloads Chrome for Testing automatically and checks again. An existing Chrome installation is used directly. Concurrent sessions wait for the same installation. The client can display checking, download, wait, completion, and failure states from native Pi extension_ui_request setStatus / notify events. During download it receives elapsed wait time; the upstream installer does not provide a reliable byte percentage. --offline prevents the automatic download and returns a clear error if Chrome is missing; it is not an operating-system network sandbox for browser page traffic. Linux ARM64 has no corresponding Chrome for Testing package, so install system Chromium in advance. If download fails, check network access and disk space, then retry the tool call. ","url":"browser.html#section-2"},{"title":"Browser interaction › Attach to an existing browser","text":" If the client has created a browser with a Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) endpoint, set AGENT_BROWSER_CDP in the Agent process environment or use agent_browser with connect <port> / --cdp <port> . Subsequent calls reuse the attached session and skip the local Chrome installation check. The client enables CDP, limits endpoint access, and owns the browser it created. A CDP endpoint grants full browser control; expose it only to trusted processes. Without an existing attachment, agent-browser starts a separate headless managed browser by default; it does not control the user’s open browser window. To observe a new window, set AGENT_BROWSER_HEADED=1 before the first launch, or use --headed with sessionMode: fresh in the first tool call. An existing headless session does not become visible automatically. Agent tool support does not imply that a particular client already provides a browser view or controls. ","url":"browser.html#section-3"},{"title":"Browser interaction › How clients integrate","text":" Clients use the existing Pi JSONL RPC; no extra tunmo.* browser command is needed. Start one Agent process per task, wait for tunmo-agent ready in an extension_ui_request / notify event, then send an ordinary prompt : {\"id\":\"browser-task-1\",\"type\":\"prompt\",\"message\":\"Open https://example.com in the browser and capture an interactive snapshot.\"} Read tool_execution_start , tool_execution_update , tool_execution_end , and agent_end as JSONL events. Use toolName to identify browser tools and read text, images, or artifacts from the result. In extension_ui_request , setStatus with statusKey: tunmo.browser.setup displays or clears setup progress; notify reports completion and errors. stderr contains diagnostics only. If the client creates a dedicated browser window, enable its CDP endpoint before starting the Agent and pass it in AGENT_BROWSER_CDP . The client owns the window display and lifecycle. The current RPC lets the Agent call tools while processing a prompt; a client cannot send a direct type: agent_browser command. For deterministic address bars, buttons, or live views, the client should control its own window and provide the UI. ","url":"browser.html#section-4"},{"title":"Browser interaction › Search credentials and validation scope","text":" Opening pages, snapshots, clicks, form fills, and screenshots do not require an Exa or Brave Search API key. The extension’s agent_browser_web_search is a separate optional tool enabled only when its credentials are configured. Tunmo’s web_search continues to use its own Web Access configuration. pnpm test:offline covers local extension registration, command calls, and browser setup with controlled fixtures. It does not validate a live site, an actual Chrome download, or a specific client’s CDP integration. The full offline suite passed on macOS ARM64 and Windows ARM64 with Node 24.0.0, 24.21.0, 25.9.0, and 26.10.0 on 2026-09-28. Future untested Node versions still require separate validation. ","url":"browser.html#section-5"},{"title":"Goal mode","text":"Set an objective for ongoing work, check progress, and continue after a pause.","url":"goals.html"},{"title":"Goal mode › Create a goal","text":" Goal mode bundles a pinned version of pi-goal and uses native Pi commands, tools, events, and Session Entries to store state. /goal --tokens 100k Prepare release notes for the current project and verify every acceptance result Send this command in the message field of a normal RPC prompt. The token budget constrains further execution based on cumulative usage for the goal; the final model call may exceed it. It is neither a cost limit nor a promise of completion. Results depend on the model, tools, and external conditions. ","url":"goals.html#section-1"},{"title":"Goal mode › Manage a goal","text":" /goal status /goal stop /goal resume /goal edit The complete revised goal /goal clear status View the current goal. In RPC mode, the result is delivered through a native extension notification. stop / resume Stopping saves the paused state and ends the active Agent turn; resume continues work on the goal. edit Supply the complete revised goal text. clear Delete the current goal. Use stop for a temporary pause. ","url":"goals.html#section-2"},{"title":"Goal mode › Completion, waiting, and recovery","text":" The Agent uses goal_complete to mark completion, goal_blocked to indicate that it cannot proceed, and goal_wait to wait when the active goal contract permits it and an external wake-up or a safe deadline condition is in place. Goal mode does not maintain a separate database; its state belongs to the current Pi Session. After a restart, reopen the original Session through Pi's session recovery mechanism. Creating a new task does not restore the goal. The client still maintains one process per task. ","url":"goals.html#section-3"},{"title":"Skills and knowledge reuse","text":"Organize repeatable workflows into discoverable, maintainable Skill Directories.","url":"skills.html"},{"title":"Skills and knowledge reuse › What a Skill does","text":" A Skill provides task instructions, steps, and supporting resources; tools perform the actual operations. The built-in tunmo-agent Skill guides the Agent in using project capabilities, while skill-creator helps create a standard Skill directory. Use the native get_commands command to find skill:* entries. User resources follow Pi's discovery mechanism for project and user directories. ","url":"skills.html#section-1"},{"title":"Skills and knowledge reuse › Create a Skill","text":" Run the following from the repository's skills/skill-creator directory: node scripts/create-skill.mjs \\ --name release-notes \\ --description \"Creates release notes from verified changes. Use for release note requests.\" \\ --when \"A verified change list is available.\" \\ --step \"Group changes by user impact and write release notes.\" \\ --constraint \"Do not invent changes or compatibility claims.\" \\ --output ./generated-skills node scripts/validate-skill.mjs ./generated-skills/release-notes The generator uses Node's built-in modules and requires no network access. Names must use lowercase kebab-case. The references, scripts, and assets directories are created only when actual files are supplied. The validator checks the directory structure, referenced paths, and some patterns associated with sensitive content. It does not execute scripts and is neither a security sandbox for arbitrary code nor a complete YAML syntax validator. Before running supporting scripts, make sure you understand what they do. ","url":"skills.html#section-2"},{"title":"Skills and knowledge reuse › Install and verify","text":" Place the complete, validated directory in ~/.tunmo/agent/skills/ , restart the Agent, and check for skill:release-notes . Copy the files referenced by SKILL.md along with SKILL.md itself. ","url":"skills.html#section-3"},{"title":"Skills and knowledge reuse › Skills bundled with extensions","text":" Starting with Host 0.1.4, extensions can declare bundled Skills in their manifests. When an extension is installed, enabled, and has its dependencies ready, new sessions automatically load the Skill for the selected extension version. qgis-runtime and geopandas-runtime explain how to use the existing tools and task scripts. A Skill does not register tools itself: gis_execute_python and geopandas_execute_python come from their respective extension versions. Loading a Skill does not expand QGIS's controlled algorithm allowlist. Disabling a module revokes tool execution, but any Skill content already loaded in an existing session remains in its context. New sessions refresh the list. Older plugin installations must first be updated through module management and load the new tools once; task scripts can then be written or modified within the current session. Do not copy a module's bundled Skill into the global directory, as this would bypass its loading lifecycle. ","url":"skills.html#section-4"},{"title":"Optional module management","text":"Review and confirm a plan before installing a complete runtime environment. Keep installation separate from everyday tool calls.","url":"modules.html"},{"title":"Optional module management › Inspect current modules","text":" node bin/tunmo-module.mjs list node bin/tunmo-module.mjs status qgis node bin/tunmo-module.mjs status geopandas list and status read local state without refreshing the Catalog over the network. The default module registry currently includes qgis and geopandas , each with its own trusted Catalog and runtime. A registered module is not necessarily installed or enabled. Before running from source, initialize the private plugin submodules for which you have read access. When loading the default registry, the module CLI reads both extension manifests; this cannot succeed if the submodule directories are empty. ","url":"modules.html#section-1"},{"title":"Optional module management › Enable QGIS for the first time","text":" node bin/tunmo-module.mjs plan qgis # Present the plan and obtain user confirmation; replace PLAN_ID with the returned planId. node bin/tunmo-module.mjs enable qgis --confirm PLAN_ID --events node bin/tunmo-module.mjs check qgis plan validates the trusted Catalog and matches the system, architecture, and version requirements of the actual Node process. It fetches metadata only and does not download the runtime. The client displays the version, download size, and peak disk usage, then returns the confirmed planId unchanged. A plan is valid for one hour. If the saved plan, extension source, or actual platform changes, or if the plan expires, run plan again and obtain confirmation. A newer release in the remote Catalog does not automatically replace the target in an already confirmed plan. After installation succeeds, start a new Agent process to load the tools. To enable GeoPandas, replace qgis with geopandas in the commands above. Its supported platforms and installation plan are matched independently. ","url":"modules.html#section-2"},{"title":"Optional module management › Everyday lifecycle operations","text":" The following are independent management operations. Choose the one that matches your current need: node bin/tunmo-module.mjs disable qgis Disable the module and request that its workers stop, while keeping the runtime. node bin/tunmo-module.mjs enable qgis Re-enable an installed module whose content has not changed, reusing the local runtime after verification. node bin/tunmo-module.mjs check qgis Check the installed runtime's content and health. node bin/tunmo-module.mjs cancel qgis Request cancellation of an ongoing management operation, then wait for the original command to exit and query its status. node bin/tunmo-module.mjs rollback qgis Switch to the previous successful binding. A usable previous version must exist. node bin/tunmo-module.mjs uninstall qgis Uninstall the module and attempt to reclaim its artifacts. Workspaces, sessions, persistent artifacts, and the download cache are retained. Shared runtimes and runtimes held by leases are not reclaimed immediately. Before updating, fetch a plan separately: node bin/tunmo-module.mjs plan qgis Present the plan and obtain confirmation. Replace PLAN_ID with the planId just confirmed, then run: node bin/tunmo-module.mjs update qgis --confirm PLAN_ID --events Repair also requires a newly generated and confirmed plan; replace update above with repair . Updates and repairs preserve the original enabled or disabled state. After a failure, query the actual state instead of inferring whether changes were committed from a client timeout alone. After installation, update, rollback, or re-enabling, create a new Agent session to load the corresponding version. ","url":"modules.html#section-3"},{"title":"Optional module management › Progress and status","text":" Successful management operations return one JSON object on stdout; help returns help text. With --events , stderr emits module-progress JSONL. Failures return a nonzero exit code and module-error . Current progress stages include planning, downloading, verifying, extracting, installing, and checking, followed by completed, failed, or cancelled. Updates and repairs use the same stages; distinguish the management action using operation.action returned by status. Cache reuse may skip downloading. Rely on the final result and a subsequent status query; a client timeout does not necessarily mean installation failed. enabled and available The former represents the enablement policy; the latter represents resource availability. They are independent. health The result of the latest check. A status query does not necessarily scan all files again. updateAvailable A comparison against the most recently saved plan, not an automatic background check over the network. restartSession When true, create a new Agent session. reload does not rebuild managed snapshots. ","url":"modules.html#section-4"},{"title":"Optional module management › Disabling and cancellation","text":" Disabling revokes execution permissions for old snapshots and stops module workers, but a stop request does not mean the processes have already exited. If the result contains workersStopped: false or state: stopping , continue querying status. After re-enabling, create a new session; old snapshots do not regain execution permissions. ","url":"modules.html#section-5"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing","text":"Use managed PyQGIS workers to inspect data, run spatial analysis, and produce traceable result files.","url":"qgis.html"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing › Before you begin","text":" QGIS is neither installed nor enabled by default. Follow the module management workflow to confirm and enable the runtime, then create a new Agent session. Environments are distributed separately for each platform, with no fallback to system QGIS, Conda, or Python for another architecture. The current QGIS extension, version 0.4.1, provides 11 gis_* tools and 5 compatibility aliases. Each call starts a supervised worker that exits on completion. The bundled qgis-runtime Skill can help you choose the right tool. ","url":"qgis.html#section-1"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing › Discover data and algorithms","text":" gis_runtime_status Inspect the currently bound Python, PyQGIS, and providers. This does not replace the management status command for an uninstalled module. gis_inspect_dataset Use input to inspect the type, CRS, fields, feature count, or raster dimensions. gis_list_algorithms Query registered algorithms by provider and see whether controlled execution is allowed. gis_describe_algorithm Use algorithm_id to retrieve parameter and output definitions. ","url":"qgis.html#section-2"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing › Spatial analysis tools","text":" gis_buffer input and distance; optional segments, dissolve, and output. Distance uses the input CRS units. gis_clip Clip a layer using input and overlay, with optional output. gis_reproject Transform the coordinate system using input and target_crs, with optional output. gis_merge_layers An inputs array, with target_crs and output as needed. gis_calculate_area A polygon input, with field_name and output as needed. Calculates WGS84 ellipsoidal area in square meters; the default field is area_m2. gis_run_algorithm algorithm_id, controlled inputs, and an optional outputs.OUTPUT filename. Usage note gis_run_algorithm currently allows only native:buffer, native:clip, native:reprojectlayer, and native:mergevectorlayers. Listing an algorithm does not mean this controlled tool is allowed to run it. Task Python can call other algorithms actually present in the runtime, but you must verify their parameters and results yourself. Starting with 0.4.1, buffering, clipping, reprojection, merging, area calculation, and their corresponding controlled entry points validate each input layer's source CRS before creating artifacts. An invalid CRS returns SourceCrsInvalid . Dataset inspection can still read layers without a CRS, and a valid custom CRS does not need an EPSG code. Task scripts must validate the CRS and operation results themselves. ","url":"qgis.html#section-3"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing › Example: create a buffer","text":" Place the input file inside the current project's workspace and inspect its CRS. For distances in meters, first reproject it into a suitable local projected coordinate system with meter units. Then ask the Agent: Check the coordinate system of roads.gpkg. After confirming that its distance units are meters, create a 100-meter buffer, save it as roads-buffer.gpkg, and report the result path. Example tool parameters: {\"input\":\"roads.gpkg\",\"distance\":100,\"output\":\"roads-buffer.gpkg\"} This example assumes the input already uses an appropriate projected CRS in meters. A value of 100 in a geographic coordinate system does not mean 100 meters. ","url":"qgis.html#section-4"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing › File and result boundaries","text":" Inputs to dataset inspection, fixed spatial tools, and gis_run_algorithm must be inside the current workspace. GeoJSON, GeoPackage, Shapefile, and GeoTIFF can be inspected; individual algorithms also require the appropriate layer type. These controlled entry points reject external paths, URLs, database URIs, VRT, SQL, scripts, and arbitrary expressions. The next section describes execution boundaries for task Python. Outputs accept plain GeoJSON or GeoPackage filenames and are written to .tunmo-gis/<executionId>/ without overwriting existing user files. Results of ≤16 MiB also provide an Artifact reference; larger files retain a workspace-relative path. Only a successful terminal state counts as a completed result. Forced termination may leave partial files. The five legacy names qgis_process_status/list/help/run and qgis_native_buffer remain as compatibility aliases, but use the new worker parameters and result boundaries. ","url":"qgis.html#section-5"},{"title":"QGIS spatial processing › Run task scripts in the current session","text":" gis_execute_python accepts a script path to a UTF-8 .py file in the workspace and an optional JSON object, params . The script defines run(context, params) and uses PyQGIS, GDAL, or Processing already available in the currently bound runtime. Each call reads a fresh script snapshot, so you can edit and rerun it in the current session. context provides the workspace, input path validation, a separate output directory, progress reporting, and cancellation. The script returns a JSON object and declares GeoJSON or GeoPackage artifacts from the current execution directory in outputs . Scripts are limited to 256 KiB, parameters to 64 KiB, and execution to 15 minutes. Ordinary Python runs with the current user's permissions. The path checks provided by context do not sandbox the entire interpreter's file or network access. Run only authorized tasks and keep the shared runtime read-only. Older plugins must first be updated through module management and load the new tools once; subsequent task script changes require neither reinstalling the extension nor restarting the session. ","url":"qgis.html#section-6"},{"title":"GeoPandas vector analysis","text":"An independent vector processing extension for spatial joins, file conversion, controlled scripts, and task Python in the current session.","url":"geopandas.html"},{"title":"GeoPandas vector analysis › Current availability","text":" Usage note The default module registry includes geopandas and its trusted Catalog. GeoPandas is disabled by default. Follow the module management workflow to generate a plan, confirm installation, and enable it, then create a new Agent session to load the tools. The current extension version is 0.2.0. Installation eligibility is determined by the current signed Catalog, the Agent's Node architecture, the operating system version, and plan . Historical platform validation belongs to the corresponding release records. This source review did not download runtimes again or repeat cross-platform GIS validation. ","url":"geopandas.html#section-1"},{"title":"GeoPandas vector analysis › Nine tools","text":" geopandas_runtime_status Inspect the versions of bundled CPython, GeoPandas, GDAL, GEOS, PROJ, and other components. geopandas_inspect_dataset source and optional layer; inspect vector data, CRS, fields, and bounds. geopandas_read_file source and optional layer / max_rows (0–100); return a bounded preview. geopandas_buffer input and distance, with optional layer / dissolve / output. Requires a projected CRS and rejects distances in longitude/latitude coordinates. geopandas_reproject input and target_crs, with optional layer / output. geopandas_spatial_join input and overlay with matching CRS; how is left/right/inner, with optional op and output. geopandas_write_file input and output, with optional layer; write GeoJSON or GeoPackage. geopandas_run_script code, with optional input / output; combine controlled data operations. geopandas_execute_python script and optional params; run task Python from the current workspace in the bound runtime. ","url":"geopandas.html#section-2"},{"title":"GeoPandas vector analysis › Controlled scripts","text":" Supported features include assignment, JSON literals, lists and dictionaries, and read, write, summarize, len, buffer, reproject, spatial_join, and head. DataFrames expose only to_crs, head, and copy. gdf = read(\"projected-points.gpkg\") gdf = buffer(gdf, 100) write(gdf, \"buffer.gpkg\") result = summarize(gdf) The example input must have an appropriate projected CRS. Pass this code as the code parameter of geopandas_run_script; do not run it in system Python. Usage note This is an explicitly defined subset of Python syntax, not a complete Python sandbox. It does not support import, loops, arbitrary attributes, network APIs, pip, or general Python functions. The entire AST is validated before interpreted execution; eval/exec are not used. ","url":"geopandas.html#section-3"},{"title":"GeoPandas vector analysis › Execution and results","text":" GeoPandas uses its own independent runtime. It does not depend on QGIS or install dependencies during tool calls. Each call starts a worker supervised by the Host, without inheriting the model API key. Fixed tools and geopandas_run_script validate that inputs are inside the trusted workspace. Task Python uses context for validated data reads and writes. Results are written to .tunmo-geopandas/<executionId>/ . Plain filenames produce GeoJSON or GeoPackage files, and files of ≤16 MiB also provide persistent artifact references. Module enablement, disabling, cancellation, and session recreation follow the shared module management workflow. ","url":"geopandas.html#section-4"},{"title":"GeoPandas vector analysis › Run task scripts in the current session","text":" When fixed tools and controlled scripts cannot express your task, create a UTF-8 .py file in the workspace, define run(context, params) , and pass its script path and optional params to geopandas_execute_python . Use libraries already present in the runtime, such as GeoPandas, Shapely, and pandas, for dissolve, overlay, and field calculations. context.read , context.write , context.summarize , and context.progress provide input validation, GeoJSON or GeoPackage artifact registration, summaries, and progress reporting. Return a plain JSON object. Each call reads a fresh code snapshot. Scripts are limited to 256 KiB, parameters to 64 KiB, and execution to 15 minutes. Ordinary Python runs with the current user's permissions; context validation does not sandbox the entire interpreter. Run only authorized tasks and do not modify the shared runtime. Existing 0.1.x installations must be updated and load 0.2.0 once. After that, editing scripts does not require restarting the session. The existing geopandas_run_script allowlist remains unchanged. ","url":"geopandas.html#section-5"},{"title":"Sessions and RPC integration","text":"Clients communicate with the Agent through Pi native JSONL. Management operations use separate CLIs.","url":"rpc.html"},{"title":"Sessions and RPC integration › Processes and channels","text":" Each client task is bound to one Agent process / Pi Session. Pass paths using argument arrays with spawn or execFile instead of concatenating shell commands. import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'; const child = spawn(process.execPath, [ '/absolute/tunmo-agent/bin/tunmo-agent.mjs', '--session-id', 'client-task-001', '--cwd', '/absolute/project', ], { stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'] }); // Wait for the ready notification before writing commands to stdin. // Frame stdout by newlines; handle diagnostics on stderr separately. Production distributions first locate and validate the bundled Node through src/bootstrap.mjs. The example above assumes a Node environment that meets the project's version requirements. It does not assume that Electron's process.execPath points to a standalone Node executable. For source development, follow Quickstart to prepare Node.js ≥24 and the locked dependencies. Assign a unique --session-id to each process. The Agent data directory is fixed to the current user’s ~/.tunmo/agent . Model calls also require a valid provider and model configuration; process readiness does not confirm model connectivity. ","url":"rpc.html#section-1"},{"title":"Sessions and RPC integration › Common messages","text":" {\"id\":\"state-1\",\"type\":\"get_state\"} {\"id\":\"commands-1\",\"type\":\"get_commands\"} {\"id\":\"prompt-1\",\"type\":\"prompt\",\"message\":\"Inspect the data files in the current project.\"} {\"id\":\"abort-1\",\"type\":\"abort\"} These are separate message examples. End each command with LF ( \\n ). Decode stdout incrementally as UTF-8 and split only on LF, accepting CRLF as well. A data chunk can contain a partial record or several records: do not JSON.parse each data chunk or use a generic line reader that also splits Unicode line separators. Continuously consume stdout and handle stdin backpressure. Register event listeners first, then wait for the following readiness notification. Check type , method , and the tunmo-agent ready prefix in message together; other notify events do not indicate readiness. {\"type\":\"extension_ui_request\",\"method\":\"notify\",\"message\":\"tunmo-agent ready；sessionId=client-task-001\",\"notifyType\":\"info\",\"id\":\"...\"} response : correlate by a unique request id and inspect success and error ; do not rely on response order. A successful prompt response only confirms acceptance, queuing, or command handling. Continue reading message and tool events; provider failures or cancellation can appear later. tool_execution_update : read tool progress; structured managed Worker progress appears in partialResult.details.execution . tool_execution_end : inspect isError and result . Managed failure details appear in result.details.errorType / result.details.execution . agent_end : one low-level run ended, but retries, compaction recovery, or queued work can follow. agent_settled means no automatic work remains for the session-level run; it does not imply every tool succeeded. The complete protocol is defined by the pinned Pi 0.87.1. After installing dependencies, consult node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/rpc.md , rpc-commands.md , and json.md . ","url":"rpc.html#section-2"},{"title":"Sessions and RPC integration › Keep management protocols separate","text":" The Agent does not provide private tunmo.* RPC or intercept or rewrite Pi native commands. Run bin/tunmo-extension.mjs for extension management and bin/tunmo-module.mjs for optional modules. These commands must not be written to Agent stdin. get_commands exposes commands and Skills; it is not an interface for retrieving complete tool schemas. Pi's new_session, switch_session, fork, and clone retain their native semantics. Parallel client tasks should each create their own process instead of using session switching as a substitute for task isolation. ","url":"rpc.html#section-3"},{"title":"Sessions and RPC integration › Exit behavior and errors","text":" stdin EOF causes a normal exit. SIGTERM and SIGHUP retain Pi's exit-code semantics of 143 and 129. Invalid startup arguments return 2; configuration initialization failure returns 20; built-in resource verification failure returns 21; a required Host extension that is not ready returns 22; and an internal launcher error returns 70. For other stages, assess stderr together with the actual exit code. Cleanup occurs between an abort request and the child process actually ending. Clients should wait for a terminal state and distinguish partial output from successful or failed results. A cancellation acknowledgement does not mean artifacts were produced successfully. ","url":"rpc.html#section-4"},{"title":"Sessions and RPC integration › Client integration checks","text":" Confirm startup and the ready notification. Set a startup deadline and handle process error / close events so the client cannot wait indefinitely. Send get_state and get_commands and check response IDs. This step requires no model call. Send /tunmo-extensions or /tunmo-runtimes through a native prompt to inspect diagnostics, consuming extension UI notifications. Readiness only guarantees required extensions are ready; optional extensions can still fail to load. After configuring a model, invoke the Hello example and inspect the tool name, input, result, and agent_settled . Also verify abort, stdin EOF, and unexpected exits end client waits and release processes. Distinguish an installation marked ready in the management store from a tool activated in the current process. Verify additions, upgrades, and newly resolved dependencies in a new Agent process. ","url":"rpc.html#section-5"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK","text":"Start with Hello: load a development package, package and install it, verify a real tool call, then choose the runtime and executor you need.","url":"extensions.html"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Create a development package","text":" Run these commands from the Agent repository root after preparing Node.js ≥24, pnpm ≥12, and dependencies as described in Quickstart . Replace placeholder paths with local absolute paths. The output parent directory must exist; creation refuses to overwrite an existing directory. node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs create org.example.hello /absolute/new/hello node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs validate extension /absolute/new/hello node bin/tunmo-agent.mjs --session-id hello-dev-001 --cwd /absolute/project --extension /absolute/new/hello/index.mjs The complete package contains extension.json , index.mjs , tools.mjs , and a local sdk.mjs . It can be copied independently without globally installing an SDK. The last command starts an RPC process. Wait for ready as described in RPC integration , then send this message through the client, terminated by a newline: {\"id\":\"hello-1\",\"type\":\"prompt\",\"message\":\"Call org_example_hello_hello with name set to World.\"} A configured, working model is required. Check tool_execution_start for toolName: org_example_hello_hello and args: {\"name\":\"World\"} . Match its toolCallId to tool_execution_end and confirm isError: false , result text Hello, World , and details.ok: true . A natural-language reply alone does not prove execution. After editing the development directory, stop the old process and start with a new session-id. Test development loading and managed installation separately for the same example. ","url":"extensions.html#section-1"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Declare tools","text":" extension.json declares the package identity, compatibility range, tool contributions, and runtimeRequirements. The entry point submits its definition through defineExtension: import { defineExtension } from './sdk.mjs'; import { hello } from './tools.mjs'; export default defineExtension(import.meta.url, () => ({ tools: { hello }, })); Keys in tools correspond to definitionExport in the manifest, and tool names correspond to the manifest name. Descriptions, parameter schemas, promptSnippet, and execution functions are maintained in the tool definition. An optional setup(pi) registers related commands and events. It must not bypass the manifest by calling registerTool again. The Host commits staged registrations only after full validation; a failed setup does not commit partial contributions. runtimeRequirements declares dependencies by alias, and a tool’s runtimeRefs may only reference those aliases. A local-in-process tool implements execute ; a local-process tool implements process and can transform its result with toResult . Use sdk/index.d.mts as the interface contract instead of importing private Host internals. Hello’s host Node requirement of >=22.19.0 describes the extension’s compatibility range. It does not lower the Agent’s current startup requirement of >=24.0.0 . ","url":"extensions.html#section-2"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Distribute and install","text":" node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs pack extension /absolute/new/hello /absolute/hello.tunmo node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs install /absolute/hello.tunmo node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs status node bin/tunmo-agent.mjs --session-id hello-installed-001 --cwd /absolute/project Inspect current.status in the install result and the extension’s enabled , current.status , and current.error in status. It should be enabled and ready. Repeat the Hello call in a new process without --extension to verify loading from the installation. The default installation scope is the user’s ~/.tunmo/agent . For a project-only installation, add --project /absolute/project to management commands and use that project as the Agent’s --cwd . --agent-dir selects the management command’s data directory only; it does not change the launcher’s fixed Agent directory. Do not mistake an isolated test store for the normal launcher’s installation store. Production management commands should also set --release-root /absolute/agent . Management provides enable, disable, rollback, forget-rollback, uninstall, gc, and recover. Bump the version before packaging and installing changes to an installed package. The same ID and version with different content is rejected, even after uninstalling. Missing runtimes cause pending-dependencies . If an upgrade candidate lacks dependencies, the previous successful version remains; inspect candidateVersion . User extensions cannot declare themselves mandatory or claim reserved tool names. Start a new Agent process after changing development files; reload retains the current session’s locked contributions. ","url":"extensions.html#section-3"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Execution and artifacts","text":" In-process tools use getExecutionContext() to access the current signal, artifact service, and authorized credentials. Subprocess tools return a process plan, and the Host locates the declared runtime. They cannot specify an arbitrary executable or fall back to PATH. The Host calls toResult after the process tree has stopped and before deleting temporary directories, allowing the tool to inspect regular files and save persistent artifacts. Do not deliver temporary paths as long-term results. The default size limit is 16 MiB per artifact. The SDK and digest verification do not provide a sandbox. In-process extensions run with the current user's permissions, and cancellation is cooperative. Load only trusted extensions. For one-off data tasks in an existing GIS module, prefer its corresponding execute_python tool. Create a new extension only when you need ongoing distribution, fixed tool parameters, or independent version management. In process(input, context) , packageRoot is the extension directory, projectWorkspace is the actual project selected at startup, and executionId is assigned by the Host. The workspace in toResult(result, { workspace }) is the temporary task directory. Keep these paths distinct and do not replace trusted context with model input. See Runtimes and execution lifecycle for runtime preparation and process protocols. ","url":"extensions.html#section-4"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Choose an example","text":" These directories are under examples/ in the Agent repository. All accept {\"name\":\"World\"} . Apply the pack / install flow above, replacing the package directory and archive filename for each example. hello : a minimal in-process tool. Call org_example_hello_hello for Hello, World . It uses the actual Agent Node and needs no separately imported Node artifact. cloud-text : text and artifact services. Call org_example_cloud_text_hello for HELLO, WORLD and an artifact reference. It runs locally by default; explicitly enable the mock backend through the execution policy . shared-node-a : subprocess tool org_example_shared_node_a_hello , returning Hello a, World , the Node version, PID, and task directory. shared-node-b : subprocess tool org_example_shared_node_b_hello , returning Hello b, World . It shares a compatible interpreter artifact with a; each invocation has its own process and task directory. Both shared-node examples require an artifact binding to org.nodejs.node@^26.0.0 and do not bundle Node. Having Node on the system PATH can still produce RuntimeNotFound . Prepare a matching artifact through offline import , then start a new process. ","url":"extensions.html#section-5"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Bundle Skills with an extension","text":" Host 0.1.4 and later support these fields in extension.json (fragment). Create the referenced SKILL.md and retain the Pi compatibility field and the rest of the manifest: { \"compatibility\": { \"extensionHost\": \"^0.1.4\", \"pi\": \"0.87.1\" }, \"skills\": [\"skills/my-skill/SKILL.md\"] } Paths must refer to readable files inside the package; duplicate, missing, or escaping paths are rejected. Package the Skill and its referenced resources with the extension so content digests, installation versions, and leases cover them. Automatic loading applies to enabled, dependency-ready extensions in managed installations and release snapshots. For development loading through --extension alone, also pass --skill /absolute/new/hello/skills/my-skill/SKILL.md . In a new process, use get_commands to check the corresponding skill:* entry. Disabling does not remove text already loaded in an old session; new sessions refresh the list. See Skills and knowledge reuse for the format. ","url":"extensions.html#section-6"},{"title":"Extensions and SDK › Verification and common issues","text":" Manifest validation: validate checks the manifest; it does not prove exports, registration, or business execution work. Packaging and installation: pack confirms packaging only. A ready result from install / status means installation dependencies resolve; also check enabled. New-process activation: after ready, inspect tool names and failures using /tunmo-extensions . For missing tools, check module, definitionExport, tool name, compatibility, and installation scope. Real invocation: inspect tool_execution_end.isError , text, and details. For artifact tools, also verify the reference can be read. A model repeating the expected text is not an execution check. RuntimeNotFound / pending-dependencies : supply a matching runtime . Same-version content conflict: bump the extension version. Old behavior after editing: start a new process and check the loaded source. Windows subprocess ExecutorUnavailable : check supervisor preparation . The repository’s test/rpc/managed-native.integration.test.mjs uses a deterministic local driver to verify real installation and tool calls offline, without a model service. ","url":"extensions.html#section-7"},{"title":"Runtimes and execution lifecycle","text":"Share complete environments by content identity and bind every session to a traceable runtime version.","url":"runtimes.html"},{"title":"Runtimes and execution lifecycle › What is a runtime?","text":" A runtime is a verified collection of interpreters, libraries, and entry points. Multiple extensions can reuse the same content. Environments with different digests or incompatible versions are kept separately; sharing does not merge site-packages directories. binding: host validates the actual Node.js runtime used by the Agent, while binding: artifact selects an artifact on disk. Matching considers the version, operating system, architecture, ABI, minimum OS version, build identity, and digest. host validates the Node actually running the Agent; importing another Node artifact does not replace it. artifact requires a verified runtime in release resources or the management store. System Node, Homebrew, and PATH are not fallback sources. A manifest alias such as node is package-local; identity is determined by runtimeId , version, and digests. ","url":"runtimes.html#section-1"},{"title":"Runtimes and execution lifecycle › Session snapshots and leases","text":" A session locks its dependencies and acquires leases when it starts. After an upgrade, existing sessions retain their previous bindings, while new sessions select the new version. Garbage collection rechecks installations, rollback references, and active leases; only unreferenced resources can be deleted. Revoking permissions when an optional module is disabled is independent of retaining its files. Keeping an old runtime does not mean an existing session can still execute a disabled module. ","url":"runtimes.html#section-2"},{"title":"Runtimes and execution lifecycle › Offline import","text":" Use a prepared, digest-sealed runtime for the target platform. An ordinary Node installation directory is not valid import-runtime input. For a complete runtime directory, validate it, then package and import it: node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs validate runtime /absolute/prepared/runtime node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs pack runtime /absolute/prepared/runtime /absolute/node.runtime.tunmo node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs import-runtime /absolute/node.runtime.tunmo node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs status You may also run import-runtime /absolute/prepared/runtime directly. If a trusted source supplies a matching .tunmo package, import it directly; validate accepts a directory, not an archive. Import performs content verification and any health check declared in the manifest. To build a Node artifact from the Agent repository, obtain the official archive selected for your current platform in resources/runtime-builds.json , then run the build command below. Its SHA-256 must match the locked configuration, and the output directory must not already exist: node scripts/build-node-runtime.mjs /absolute/downloads/node-archive /absolute/new/node-runtime-build node bin/tunmo-extension.mjs import-runtime /absolute/new/node-runtime-build/node.runtime.tunmo Replace node-archive with the actual archive named in the configuration. The build configuration currently pins Node 26.0.0 and provides darwin-arm64 and win32-arm64 targets. The script selects the current process platform; it is not a cross-compiler and does not establish support for other platforms. Outputs include runtime/ , node.runtime.tunmo , and report.json . Runtime versions are independent of documentation-site versions. Both shared-node examples require org.nodejs.node@^26.0.0 . Importing a matching artifact into the same management store retries pending dependencies. Run status again to confirm ready, then start a new Agent process. Importing a newer runtime alone does not change the exact bindings of an already-ready installation. Management does not run npm/pip installation scripts. Large directory imports are limited to 16 GiB and 200,000 files. The v1 .tunmo format retains limits of 512 MiB unpacked and 256 MiB compressed and cannot directly package large GIS environments. ","url":"runtimes.html#section-3"},{"title":"Runtimes and execution lifecycle › Startup and cleanup","text":" A runtime can declare fixed arguments, bundled scripts, and environment settings through launch. An executable path from entrypoints alone does not replace the complete startup plan that includes launch. Each subprocess invocation has its own task directory, supervisor, and execution lease. Unix uses process groups; Windows uses Job Objects. Stop the process tree before cleaning up directories and releasing resources. A timeout does not mean cleanup has finished. The JSONL Worker protocol is separate from the external Pi RPC protocol. Worker messages must carry the same request ID, may report status and progress, and must return exactly one terminal state. Diagnostics from third-party libraries go to stderr. For structured Workers, set protocol: \"jsonl-v1\" and requestId: context.executionId in the process plan. Each message carries protocolVersion: 1 , the same id , and a type. A successful terminal message has {\"type\":\"result\",\"ok\":true,\"result\":{}} plus the protocol version and id; failures use ok:false and error:{type,message} . Emit nothing after the terminal message. Successful content reaches toResult as result.workerResult . The shared-node examples use plain stdout JSON and do not need this protocol. Before running subprocess tools from source on Windows, run node scripts/build-windows-helper.mjs in that Windows environment. Production builds include the supervisor. A missing supervisor can produce ExecutorUnavailable . Verify on the actual target platform; a successful macOS run does not verify Windows. ","url":"runtimes.html#section-4"},{"title":"Runtimes and execution lifecycle › Cloud execution boundaries","text":" examples/cloud-text uses local-in-process by default. To test the local mock backend, add this configuration to ~/.tunmo/agent/execution-policy.json and start a new Agent process. If the file exists, merge the tool entry while preserving other policies: { \"schemaVersion\": 1, \"tools\": { \"org.example.cloud-text/hello\": { \"executor\": \"cloud\", \"backend\": \"mock\", \"allowDataTransfer\": true } } } The policy key is extension ID/tool ID , here org.example.cloud-text/hello . The RPC tool name is org_example_cloud_text_hello ; do not interchange them. The tool manifest must also declare the cloud executor. Invoke with {\"name\":\"World\"} and check HELLO, WORLD , details.ok , and details.artifact . The mock path labels artifact references remote, but execution still occurs locally; no real cloud service is involved. Removing this tool policy restores local execution in new processes. Disallowed data transfer fails; a local failure never automatically uploads data or switches execution to the cloud. When a remote result is unknown, retain recovery information instead of blindly resubmitting a task with side effects. These adapters and mock tests do not establish production cloud availability. ","url":"runtimes.html#section-5"},{"title":"Troubleshooting","text":"Identify where the failure occurs, then check configuration, resources, and session state.","url":"troubleshooting.html"},{"title":"Troubleshooting › Startup fails or no ready notification arrives","text":" Check the Node.js and pnpm versions and verify that --session-id / --cwd are valid. Read stderr. Keep diagnostics separate from stdout JSONL parsing. Check dependencies, release resources, and read access to private submodules. A complete source checkout requires initializing the pinned optional-extensions revision before running pnpm check:deps. If configuration was repaired, inspect the .bak backup and restore valid fields. If a browser tool fails on first use, inspect the status and error notices in extension_ui_request . If Chrome is missing in an offline session, allow a connected session to install it automatically first. Linux ARM64 requires system Chromium. For an existing CDP browser, verify that the endpoint is reachable. If a built-in file's digest does not match, restore the matching release content. Use the project's seal-release workflow only when actively developing and intentionally resealing built-in content. Do not treat resealing as a general fix that bypasses verification. ","url":"troubleshooting.html#section-1"},{"title":"Troubleshooting › The process is ready but the model does not respond","text":" Use get_state to confirm the current state, then check the model, provider credentials, and network. The default configuration does not automatically grant access to a model. Inspect native error events; ready does not indicate successful authentication. ","url":"troubleshooting.html#section-2"},{"title":"Troubleshooting › GIS tools are missing","text":" Run tunmo-module list and confirm that the current release registers the module. Query status and check enabled, available, and health separately. Complete plan → confirmation → enable. Create a new Agent process. Re-enabling and updating a module require more than reload. The current default manifest registers both QGIS and GeoPandas. If list does not include a module, check the Agent release configuration and submodule version actually in use. The two modules are installed independently; installing QGIS does not enable GeoPandas. Updating only the submodule pointer in the main repository does not change the installed plugin version. If gis_execute_python or geopandas_execute_python is missing, check the installed version, confirm and complete the update, then create a new session. ","url":"troubleshooting.html#section-3"},{"title":"Troubleshooting › Platform, plan, or content errors","text":" RuntimeIncompatible Check the actual architecture and OS version of the Node.js runtimes used for management and for the Agent, along with the Catalog's compatibility range. Installation requires a compatible package. ConfirmationRequired Obtain a plan first, then confirm its planId. InstallationPlanExpired The plan has expired or its content has changed. Run plan again and confirm the new plan. pending-dependencies The extension has been discovered, but its runtime dependencies are not fully ready. Add the missing verified artifacts first. SourceCrsInvalid Confirm and repair the source CRS using the data's provenance before running QGIS spatial operations. Do not guess the input coordinate system from the target CRS. ExecutorUnavailable Check the supervisor and release resources required by the current platform. Windows development builds need the corresponding helper. ","url":"troubleshooting.html#section-4"},{"title":"Troubleshooting › Result files or disk space issues","text":" Locate files using workspacePath / Artifact references in successful tool results. Output from cancelled tasks may be incomplete. Uninstalling does not delete user data or the download cache, and environments still referenced by sessions are retained. End sessions normally before reclaiming resources through the management entry point. If update status has not refreshed, run plan first; status itself does not access the network. Run check if you suspect damaged resources. Historical health values do not establish that all bytes are currently intact. ","url":"troubleshooting.html#section-5"},{"title":"Versions, scope, and maintenance","text":"Use the current source checkout as the authority, distinguishing implemented capabilities, release configuration, and historical verification records.","url":"reference.html"},{"title":"Versions, scope, and maintenance › Documentation baseline","text":" Source review date: 2026-09-28. The documentation was first organized on 2026-09-17. This revision updates the main entry points and limitations against the current launcher, configuration, CLI, SDK, extension manifests, and usage contracts. package.json identifies the package as @tunmo/agent 0.0.0 , a development package that cannot be published directly to npm. It pins Pi 0.87.1 and pi-goal 0.54.4. The QGIS extension is version 0.4.1, and GeoPandas is version 0.2.0. This review does not mean that all functional tests, network verification, runtime downloads, or cross-platform GIS acceptance tests were repeated. Dated release and acceptance records establish only the scope documented at that time. A development version number is not a commitment to a stable release. ","url":"reference.html#section-1"},{"title":"Versions, scope, and maintenance › Known scope","text":" QGIS and GeoPandas are both registered in the default module manifest and disabled by default. Their runtimes are installed, enabled, and managed separately. Optional extensions come from the private tunmo-plugins Git submodule. Building from source requires read access, or a matching complete release package supplied by a maintainer. Runtime availability depends on the actual signed Catalog and plan. The documentation does not promise support for every macOS, Windows, or Linux system. An implemented Agent interface does not mean a particular desktop client already provides the corresponding UI / IPC. Cloud execution currently offers only an explicitly enabled local mock backend. Extensions and task Python code run with the current user's permissions. Module confirmation, path validation, and process supervision do not provide an operating system sandbox. ","url":"reference.html#section-2"},{"title":"Versions, scope, and maintenance › Source code and historical records","text":" The end of each page lists its source references, relative to the Agent repository root. Read them in a complete source checkout. References starting with optional-extensions/ also require read access to the corresponding private submodule. This site's tool and installation instructions are self-contained; readers do not need access to a developer's local directories or handover records. The repository publishes the nine core technical documents at the root of docs/ , along with this site's content. Design proposals, source audits, runtime package records, historical handovers, and raw verification evidence remain in categorized directories on maintainers' machines. They are excluded by .gitignore and are not included in this documentation release. Historical conclusions do not replace the current manifests and source code. ","url":"reference.html#section-3"},{"title":"Versions, scope, and maintenance › Update the documentation","text":" All site files are in docs/public. Open index.html directly or browse through any static HTTP server. The site has no external fonts, CDN resources, or runtime dependencies. content.json and content.en.json maintain the Chinese and English content respectively. Page IDs, section IDs, order, and source references must match between the two files. After editing them, run: node docs/public/build.mjs This generates 15 standalone HTML pages per language, with separate full-text search indexes and Markdown copies. Chinese pages are at the site root and English pages are under en/. The language control in the header switches the current page while preserving its section anchor. Styles and interactions are maintained in assets/styles.css and assets/app.js. See README.md in the same directory for maintenance instructions. When tools, configuration, or platform support change, review the corresponding source code instead of relying solely on old handover records. ","url":"reference.html#section-4"}];
